From 1fdc431af37359288503abf7bd8eeee5de7d7585 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hotty Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 16:35:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] blitz-dom: a <use> draws an element of another SVG resource
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`<use href="sprite.svg#icon">` drew nothing: Blitz collects only
same-document fragment references, and usvg loads no external `use`.
A `<use>` whose href names an element of another resource now has the
resource fetched through the document's NetProvider, as an inline SVG's
`<image>` is, and kept by its URL. It is fetched with the empty
destination rather than as an image: it is a document, and a provider
whose policy goes by destination (CSP's `img-src`) is not to take it
for one.

When its `<svg>` is constructed, the element it names, and what that
element refers to in its resource (`href="#…"`, `url(#…)`), is copied
into the `<svg>`'s `<defs>` under a prefix that no id of the `<svg>`
has, and the `<use>` refers to the copy. It is drawn in the context of
the `<use>`, as a same-document `<use>` is, so `currentColor` is the use
site's. The resource is parsed once, when a `<use>` first draws from it,
and each element's copy is written once and kept with it. The resource
arriving late, sent again, or the href changing constructs the `<svg>`
again.
